…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥I say then, Have they stumbled that they should fall? God forbid: but rather through their fall salvation is come unto the Gentiles, for to provoke them to jealousy.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙˆNow if the fall of them be the riches of the world, and the diminishing of them the riches of the Gentiles; how much more their fulness?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaFor I speak to you Gentiles, inasmuch as I am the apostle of the Gentiles, I magnify mine office:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`if by any means I may provoke to emulation them which are my flesh, and might save some of them.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}For if the casting away of them be the reconciling of the world, what shall the receiving of them be, but life from the dead?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcFor if the firstfruit be holy, the lump is also holy: and if the root be holy, so are the branches.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¬And if some of the branches be broken off, and thou, being a wild olive tree, wert graffed in among them, and with them partakest of the root and fatness of the olive tree;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`boast not against the branches. But if thou boast, thou bearest not the root, but the root thee.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMThou wilt say then, The branches were broken off, that I might be graffed in.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhWell; because of unbelief they were broken off, and thou standest by faith. Be not highminded, but fear:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRfor if God spared not the natural branches, take heed lest he also spare not thee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ³Behold therefore the goodness and severity of God: on them which fell, severity; but toward thee, goodness, if thou continue in his goodness: otherwise thou also shalt be cut off.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpAnd they also, if they abide not still in unbelief, shall be graffed in: for God is able to graff them in again.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙßFor if thou wert cut out of the olive tree which is wild by nature, and wert graffed contrary to nature into a good olive tree: how much more shall these, which be the natural branches, be graffed into their own olive tree?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÏFor I would not, brethren, that ye should be ignorant of this mystery, lest ye should be wise in your own conceits; that blindness in part is happened to Israel, until the fulness of the Gentiles be come in.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines“‚¤textÙ3And so all Israel shall be saved: as it is written,¦layout ‚¤textÙ+There shall come out of Sion the Deliverer,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ+And shall turn away ungodliness from Jacob:¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ"For this is my covenant unto them,¦layout ‚¤textÙ"When I shall take away their sins.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAs concerning the gospel, they are enemies for your sakes: but as touching the election, they are beloved for the fathers' sakes.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ8For the gifts and calling of God are without repentance.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbFor as ye in times past have not believed God, yet have now obtained mercy through their unbelief:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ]even so have these also now not believed, that through your mercy they also may obtain mercy.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOFor God hath concluded them all in unbelief, that he might have mercy upon all.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book,§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse$¦verses”„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…O the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and knowledge of God! how unsearchable are his judgments, and his ways past finding out!¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIFor who hath known the mind of the Lord? or who hath been his counsellor?¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙKOr who hath first given to him, and it shall be recompensed unto him again?¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYFor of him, and through him, and to him, are all things: to whom be glory for ever. Amen.¦layout ªlast_verse$